<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">1.  Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) such as GPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al. (2023)</a></cite>, Llama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and others have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across diverse natural language processing (NLP) tasks. However, their widespread adoption is hampered by their susceptibility to adversarial attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goodfellow et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. These attacks involve crafting subtle, often human-imperceptible perturbations to input text, causing LLMs to produce erroneous, biased, or harmful outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing defense strategies range from input pre-processing and adversarial training to detection mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. While effective to some extent, many of these methods lack transparency. Understanding “why” a specific input was flagged or modified is crucial for building trust, debugging models, and iterating on security measures. This is particularly important in high-stakes applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address this gap, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ExplainableGuard</span>, a novel defense mechanism that utilizes the advanced reasoning abilities of a powerful LLM, DeepSeek-Reasoner, to not only defend against adversarial attacks but also to explain its defense process. Our core contribution lies in designing a structured, Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite> prompting strategy that elicits detailed reasoning from the defense LLM. This reasoning breaks down the analysis into character, word, structural, and semantic levels, culminating in a decision, a purified text, and a comprehensive explanation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The structure of this paper is as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Related Work ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a review of prior research on adversarial attacks and defense. Section <a href="#S3" title="3. Methodology ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> describes the components of the ExplainableGuard architecture, details the Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting approach, and highlights its potential for robust and interpretable defense mechanisms. Section <a href="#S4" title="4. Experimental Setup ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> outlines the evaluation design, while Section <a href="#S5" title="5. Preliminary Results and Analysis ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> Results presents both the quantitative and qualitative findings. The Limitations of the proposed approach are discussed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. Discussion ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, and the paper concludes with final remarks in Section <a href="#S7" title="7. Conclusion ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. This work aims to demonstrate that the proposed system effectively mitigates various types of adversarial attacks while providing valuable insights into its operational logic.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">2.  Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">2.1.  Adversarial Attacks on LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adversarial attacks on LLMs involve subtle perturbations of the input text that alter the predictions of the model without affecting the human-perceived meaning of the text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Adversarial attacks can be categorized into several levels. Character-level attacks involve manipulations such as typos, homoglyphs, or invisible characters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ebrahimi et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. Word-level attacks involve replacing words with synonyms, paraphrasing sentences, or inserting/deleting words <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alzantot et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. More sophisticated attacks target sentence structure or semantics, including prompt injection and jailbreak techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. These types of attacks expose the vulnerability of current LLMs, highlighting the need for more robust defenses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">2.2.  Adversarial Defense Mechanisms</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Defense usually involves input cleaning (e.g., filtering special characters, spell checking), adversarial training (fine-tuning models of adversarial examples)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. In the field of image classification, adversarial defense also involves authentication defense that provides robustness guarantees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Croce et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Some methods employ detector models to flag malicious inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mozes et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. However, many of these defenses do not clearly explain why an input is considered adversarial or how an attack is eliminated. A similar work by Lin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> uses an LLM agent for adversarial purification and achieves satisfying performance. However, it lacks explainability, which represents a significant gap.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">2.3.  Explainable AI (XAI) in NLP</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Explainability in natural language processing (NLP) seeks to render model predictions and internal reasoning processes transparent and interpretable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Danilevsky et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Traditional approaches include attention visualization, such as AttentionViz <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yeh et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and feature-attribution methods, including LIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2016</a>)</cite> and SHAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee, 2017</a>)</cite>. More recently, natural language explanations have been introduced to generate human-readable justifications alongside model outputs, often by training on datasets annotated with explanatory comments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Danilevsky et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A promising paradigm is CoT prompting, whereby large language models are induced to produce intermediate reasoning steps that lead to a final answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. This mechanism not only provides the final output of the model, but also generates verifiable decision sequences that can be carefully examined by researchers. Our work utilizes CoT to generate explanations in the context of adversarial defense.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">3.  Methodology</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed system, ExplainableGuard, employs DeepSeek-Reasoner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as a security analyst LLM. Given potentially adversarial input text <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal is to produce a cleaned version <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{clean}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{clean}</annotation></semantics></math> and a human-readable short explanation <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> detailing the purification content. Additionally, our system will produce a reasoning process <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> containing how our LLM analysis the text, detect adversarial patterns and do the purification. The workflow of ExplainableGuard is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2511.13771v1/figure1_keynotes_1_cropped.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/133;" width="628" height="133" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Overview of the ExplainableGuard Chain-of-Thought (CoT) workflow.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">3.1.  System Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method can be formalized as a function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{E},\{0,1\},\mathcal{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:\mathcal{T}\rightarrow(\mathcal{T},\mathcal{E},\{0,1\},\mathcal{R})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the space of texts, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the space of explanations, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the space of reasoning contents. Given an adversarial input <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math>, the function outputs a tuple:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T_{clean},E,\text{is\_adv},R)=D(T_{adv})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>is_adv</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T_{clean},E,\text{is\_adv},R)=D(T_{adv})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{clean}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{clean}</annotation></semantics></math> is the purified text, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> is a concise human-readable explanation, <span id="S3.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">is_adv</span> is a boolean indicating whether the input was identified as adversarial, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is the detailed reasoning content generated by the model during the analysis and purification process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">3.2.  Chain-of-Thought Prompting for Defense</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The interpretability of ExplainableGuard is achieved through a carefully designed CoT prompt, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{CoT}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{CoT}</annotation></semantics></math>, which systematically guides DeepSeek-Reasoner through a sequence of analytical steps. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Methodology ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the prompt instructs the LLM to conduct a comprehensive assessment at multiple levels: starting with character-level inspection (e.g., detecting homoglyphs, invisible characters, typos, leetspeak), followed by word-level analysis to identify unusual synonym usage or suspicious insertions/deletions, then examining structural aspects (sentence structure anomalies or embedded commands), and finally performing semantic and contextual checks to uncover subtle meaning shifts or indirect prompt injections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After these analyses, the model determines whether the input is adversarial and formulates an appropriate purification strategy. The LLM then applies this strategy to generate the cleaned text <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{clean}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{clean}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, it produces a structured summary that includes the adversarial judgment (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">is_adv</span>), the purified text, a concise explanation <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, and a reasoning process <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">4.  Experimental Setup</h2>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2511.13771v1/figure2_keynote_cropped.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:628/141;" width="628" height="141" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The workflow of the experiments, including the generation of adversarial examples and the defense of ExplainableGuard.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">4.1.  Dataset</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted experiments on both short and long text datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GLUE Benchmark:</span> For short text evaluation, we selected three representative tasks from the GLUE benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>: SST-2, RTE, and QQP. These datasets are widely used for assessing natural language understanding and are characterized by relatively short input texts. Because of time and budget constraints, we ran a sample of the QQP dataset instead of the full validation set.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IMDB Movie Reviews:</span> For long text evaluation, we included the IMDB movie review dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maas et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, which contains lengthy user-written reviews labeled for sentiment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these datasets allow us to evaluate our method’s robustness and interpretability across diverse text lengths and task types.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">4.2.  Baselines</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare ExplainableGuard with a baseline where no defense is applied. Specifically, we evaluate the performance of a target LLM (GPT-3.5-turbo) on adversarial inputs generated by the PromptAttack method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, without any additional defense mechanism.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">4.3.  Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use three main evaluation metrics in our experiments, each serving a distinct purpose:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Success Rate (ASR):</span> ASR directly measures whether our defense can help the model make correct predictions in the presence of adversarial attacks. A lower ASR indicates that the defense is more effective at preventing the model from being fooled by adversarial inputs. It is defined as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin and Zhao, 2024</a>)</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{ASR}=\frac{|\{x\in D_{\text{correct}}:f(x^{\prime})\neq y\}|}{|D_{\text{correct}}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>ASR</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{ASR}=\frac{|\{x\in D_{\text{correct}}:f(x^{\prime})\neq y\}|}{|D_{\text{correct}}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\text{correct}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>correct</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{correct}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of samples that are correctly classified by the model on the original test dataset, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding adversarial example, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s prediction result on the adversarial example, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the true label.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BLEU Score:</span> The BLEU score is used to measure the similarity between the purified text and the original, unperturbed text <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Papineni et al., 2002</a>)</cite>. In our evaluation, we compute a weighted average of the 1-gram and 2-gram BLEU scores for each example, and then report the mean over all successfully defended examples. Let <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-gram orders considered, and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the total number of successful defense examples. The BLEU score is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BLEU}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{n\in C}w_{n}\cdot\mathrm{BLEU}_{i}^{(n)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>BLEU</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>BLEU</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BLEU}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{n\in C}w_{n}\cdot\mathrm{BLEU}_{i}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{BLEU}_{i}^{(n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>BLEU</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{BLEU}_{i}^{(n)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-gram BLEU score for the <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th example. A higher BLEU score indicates that our purification process better preserves the original content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Evaluation:</span> Beyond ASR and BLEU, we conducted a human-evaluation study to assess the interpretability of ExplainableGuard’s explanations, which is the most significant contribution of our work. We sampled 40 successful adversarial defense instances balanced across SST-2, RTE, QQP, and IMDB (10 per dataset). We created an ablated variant, EG-noCoT, which uses the same prompt but without chain-of-thought reasoning, and let it generate an explanation as well. Then, we compared ExplainableGuard’s (EG) explanations with those from EG-noCoT.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We employed a within-subjects, counterbalanced design. For each item and condition, two human annotators were shown the adversarial input, the cleaned output, and the explanation. They rated the following aspects on five-point Likert scales, with higher values indicating better performance:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clarity</span>: "I can understand why this output was produced."</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Specificity</span>: "The explanation cites concrete features such as tokens or structures."</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Actionability</span>: "The explanation would help me address similar attacks."</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conciseness</span>: "The explanation is appropriately brief for practical use."</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We additionally collected a binary deployability-trust judgment ("Would you trust deploying this defense on similar inputs?"). For the final statistic of the binary deployability-trust judgment, if at least one of the two annotators rated an item as "yes," we counted that row of data as "yes."</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_section" style="font-size:120%;">5.  Preliminary Results and Analysis</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_subsection" style="font-size:110%;">5.1.  Attack Success Rate</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.1. Attack Success Rate ‣ 5. Preliminary Results and Analysis ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents the ASR against PromptAttack-EN (PA-EN) and PromptAttack-FS-EN (PA-FS-EN) attacks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, comparing the performance of ExplainableGuard (EG) with the baseline model (GPT-3.5-turbo) without any defense. FS denotes ’Few-Shot,’ indicating that the adversarial attack employs a few-shot learning strategy to achieve higher performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across the three datasets, we observe a notable reduction in ASR when EG is applied. For instance, under the PA-EN attack, the ASR for RTE drops from 34.30% to 13.18%. The average ASR across all datasets is reduced from 37.27% to 24.21% (PA-EN) and 42.87% to 24.31% (PA-FS-EN). This indicates that EG effectively mitigates the adversarial attacks, enhancing the model’s robustness. Additionally, for IMDB dataset, the ASR without defense is 38.71%, while applying EG reduces it to 30.11% as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1. Attack Success Rate ‣ 5. Preliminary Results and Analysis ‣ ExplainableGuard: Interpretable Adversarial Defense for LLMs Using Chain-of-Thought Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. This indicates that our method is also effective for long-text adversarial defense.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:455.2pt;height:106.3pt;vertical-align:-48.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(105.5pt,-24.6pt) scale(1.86406123118273,1.86406123118273) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attacks</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EG</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SST-2</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RTE</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QQP</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Avg.</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">PA-EN</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">56.00</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">34.30</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">21.50</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">37.27</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S5.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\checkmark" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>✓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\checkmark</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">40.89</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">13.18</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">18.57</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">24.21</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">PA-FS-EN</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">75.23</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">36.12</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">17.26</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">42.87</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_b"><math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\checkmark" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>✓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\checkmark</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">48.61</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">10.32</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">14.01</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">24.31</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Performance comparison of defense methods against different attacks on SST-2, RTE, and QQP.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
